#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A registered break must still print the sentence its record says it printed.

MISE description="Replay each REDNESS row's break and check it reddens its own guard"
"""
# `mise run anchors` says a break can be STARTED: the search literal resolves to
# exactly one occurrence, so `scripts/break-once.sh` will not abort. It says nothing
# about whether the break still REDDENS, and no resolution check can — an anchor
# resolving is a property of one line, while reddening is a property of a test that
# lives somewhere else, often in another file.
#
# That gap is not theoretical. `C10`'s anchor kept resolving while another branch
# rewrote the test its record described, so the break stopped reddening and the gate
# never moved. And the bias does NOT run one way: on 2026-08-08 both records a
# reviewer challenged claimed MORE redness than exists, because an author holds the
# guards they WROTE in mind while the neighbouring guard's shape is not in that
# picture — and on the same day the registry's `N1` row went the other way. As
# challenged it named THREE affected guards, and five are affected. Its record, in
# the file its `ANCHORS` row names, names all five now and marks the hang. Both
# directions occur, so nothing here may assume a record over-claims. An over-claiming
# record wastes a reader's time; an under-claiming one makes a stalled run read as a
# broken harness — which is this whole file's failure mode, one layer down.
#
# (`N1` here is the registry row id. `keylink/src/turns.rs` uses the same two
# characters for an unrelated finding about incarnations, so the substitution is
# spelled out below rather than left to the id.)
#
# ── WHY THIS READS THE REGISTRY INSTEAD OF HARD-CODING ITS OWN BREAKS ──────────
#
# `scripts/replay-fate-breaks.sh` hard-codes twenty-three breaks, and that is the
# defect this one avoids: its literals are invisible to `mise run anchors`, so they
# rot exactly the way the records did — silently, until somebody runs the script.
# Reading `ANCHORS` means the gate keeps every literal here resolving, on every
# commit, and this task only has to supply the half a gate cannot check.
#
# The registry is PARSED, not imported. Importing would run the gate as a side
# effect, and `ast.literal_eval` on the assignment's value is exact where a regex
# over the source would be a guess — a file that exists because of unchecked guesses
# does not get to make one.
#
# ── IT ASSERTS ON THE SENTENCE AND NEVER ON THE EXIT CODE ──────────────────────
#
# `break-once.sh` always exits 0 and reports the runner's status as text, and the
# argument for keeping it that way is `replay-fate-breaks.sh`'s: a break that goes
# GREEN prints `exit=0`, an aborted anchor prints no `exit=` line at all, and a break
# red at a DIFFERENT assertion prints a different sentence. Asserting on the recorded
# sentence puts all three in the failure arm. An exit code cannot tell them apart —
# `exit=101` is identical for a break that reddens the right guard and one that
# reddens the wrong one, and the wrong one reads as evidence.
#
# One arm below now READS that line, and the paragraph above still holds. The
# declared `unaffected` arm requires the recorded sentence AND `exit=0`, because a
# `test … ok` line does not say that no SIBLING test under the same filter reddened —
# and if one did, the break WAS observed and `unaffected` is the wrong cell. That is
# an extra requirement inside one arm. It is never a substitute for the sentence, and
# no arm passes on an exit code alone.
#
# ── AND ONE ARM READS A COUNT, WHICH IS NOT A VERDICT BUT A PRECONDITION ───────
#
# The sibling in that paragraph is a whole class, and the `exit=0` test caught it in
# ONE cell only. A row names ONE guard; `cargo test` resolves a filter as a
# SUBSTRING; so a name that is a strict prefix of a sibling's selects both, and the
# recorded sentence can arrive from the test the row does NOT name. `scripts/
# break-once.sh` now counts what its filter selected — under either runner, once,
# for all three of its consumers — and prints `── selected=N`. Any N that is not 1
# refuses the row BEFORE the cells below, because it disqualifies the evidence
# rather than scoring it: with two tests under one filter, no cell is answerable.
# The `exit=0` test stays as the narrower guard that still works when the harness
# could not count, and `── selected=?` is exactly that state.
#
# ── THE TWO MODES A PASS/FAIL CELL CANNOT HOLD ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Both were MEASURED on 2026-08-08 by applying the registry's `N1` break —
# `Shared::entry`'s `mpsc::channel(self.mailbox_depth)` replaced by
# `DEFAULT_MAILBOX_DEPTH` — and running every `mailbox_depth` call site by name.
# Neither is a pass, and neither is a failure of the kind the arm above describes.
#
#   * A HANG. `buffered_is_exact_at_depth_and_counts_no_parked_sender` never
#     reported at all. Its actor consumes nothing, so at one slot the second of
#     three `mail.send(..).await`s parks forever — BEFORE the assertion that would
#     have caught the wrong count. It was killed by hand at 120 s. There is no
#     in-test timeout, so the runner waits until the replay harness's outer
#     deadline classifies the hang.
#
#     **A hang is the break being OBSERVED, not the harness being broken.** A guard
#     that stops reporting under a substitution felt that substitution. Scoring it
#     as an error hides a working break. So a row is killed on a wall clock and the
#     kill is scored as evidence — and the summary says the row's recorded SENTENCE
#     went unchecked, because it did.
#
#   * A GUARD THE BREAK REACHES AND DOES NOT MOVE.
#     `ten_thousand_idle_actors_fit_in_the_budget` sets three depths and PASSES
#     under `N1` with byte-identical output: 1738 bytes at depths 1, 32 and 1,024 on
#     the honest tree, and 1738 under the break. Its assertions demand that the
#     three depths cost the SAME bytes, so flattening all three to one slot
#     satisfies them. A one-sided assertion cannot see a break that removes the
#     variable it compares across. The guard is correct and the break does not touch
#     what it measures, so scoring it as a missing expected failure is a FALSE
#     ALARM — and a false alarm in a checker is what gets the checker switched off.
#
# ── WHY ONE OF THE TWO IS DECLARED IN THE ROW AND THE OTHER IS INFERRED ────────
#
# An `unaffected` cell with no declared reason is indistinguishable from ROT, and rot
# is the whole thing this task exists to find. A guard rewritten until the break no
# longer reaches it goes GREEN. A guard the break honestly cannot move also goes
# GREEN. Nothing in the output separates them, so `unaffected` is not a verdict this
# task can infer — a row DECLARES it, in an optional fifth element carrying the
# reason, and the reason is printed on every run where it is used.
#
# Three things keep that declaration from becoming a place a dead guard can hide:
#
#   * An UNDECLARED green still fails, in the same arm and with the same words as
#     before. The fifth element is the only way into the `unaffected` cell.
#   * A declared row still records a SENTENCE, and it is still asserted. For a
#     declared row that sentence comes from a watched GREEN run and says the guard
#     RAN — `cargo test` with a filter that matches NOTHING also exits 0, so
#     `exit=0` alone would let a deleted test read as unaffected. Declaring
#     `unaffected` therefore still costs a watched run and a line pasted out of it.
#   * A declared row that HANGS fails. The declaration says the break does not reach
#     what this guard measures, and a hang says it does.
#
# A hang needs no declaration, and that asymmetry is the argument rather than a
# convenience: rot goes GREEN, it does not hang. A hang is positive evidence that
# the substitution changed what the guard's own code does, so no dead guard can
# reach that cell.
#
# ── HOW THE KILL IS DELIVERED, WHICH IS MEASURED AND NOT A DETAIL ──────────────
#
# `subprocess.run(timeout=…)` cannot be used for this, and it is worth naming why,
# because it is the obvious thing to reach for and it does the two worst things at
# once. Measured 2026-08-08 against a scratch shell shaped like `break-once.sh` — a
# `trap restore EXIT`, then a foreground `sleep | tee` — under the bash that
# `/usr/bin/env bash` resolves to on this machine, GNU bash 3.2.57(1)-release:
#
#   * `subprocess.run(capture_output=True, timeout=1)`: the trap did NOT run, and
#     three processes survived, reparented to PID 1. So it leaves the BREAK IN THE
#     FILE and orphans the runner — and `AGENTS.md` records that orphan as an
#     incident of its own: it fires its `trap EXIT` later and copies a stale backup
#     over whatever the file holds by then.
#   * SIGKILL to the process GROUP: the trap did not run either. Nothing survived,
#     and the file stayed broken.
#   * SIGTERM to the shell PID alone: the trap RAN, and two processes survived.
#   * SIGTERM to the process GROUP: the trap ran, and nothing survived.
#
# So the order is SIGTERM to the group, then a bounded wait, then SIGKILL to the
# group only if it is still there. `start_new_session=True` is what makes the group
# knowable: the child becomes its own group leader, so the group id IS its pid, and
# nothing has to call `getpgid` after the child is reaped — that call raises
# `ProcessLookupError` once it is gone, which was measured while writing this.
#
# **And the tree is re-checked after every row.** A restore that did not happen is
# silent, and the next row would then cut a second literal into an already-broken
# file — or, for the last row, leave the break in the tree with the task reporting
# success. The check can only run because the tree was refused if dirty at the start.
#
# ── WHY THE TIMEOUT IS GENEROUS, AND WHY A MISSING START LINE FAILS ────────────
#
# A kill that lands during COMPILATION is not a hang. Scoring one as a hang would be
# this task reporting a break it never ran — a false PASS, which is the failure this
# whole file exists to prevent. Two things keep it out.
#
# The default is 600 s per row, which has to clear a COLD build rather than the 120 s
# at which the measured hang was killed by hand. `REPLAY_TIMEOUT` moves it. A genuine
# hang therefore costs the full timeout, which is the right price for a REPLAY that is
# run by hand.
#
# **Only the replay path reads this timeout**, and the sentence above said "a task that
# is run by hand and never in a gate" until `--selftest` became a gate step. That arm
# passes its own two seconds at its one `run_watched` call, so registering it put no
# 600 s wait into anybody's push. This is the third sentence in this file to be
# narrowed for the same reason, after the heading below and its closing paragraph: a
# claim about the whole file was true only of one of its two arms.
#
# And the `hang` arm requires a line saying the runner got PAST its build. `running `
# is libtest's and `Finished ` is cargo's, and both were read off the one measured
# `break-once.sh` log these fixtures are shaped on. The set is CONSERVATIVE BY
# DIRECTION: a marker it does not know costs a false FAILURE whose message names its
# own remedy, and it can never buy a false pass. For a checker that is the direction
# to be wrong in.
#
# ── THE CLASSIFIER IS A PURE FUNCTION, AND `--selftest` IS WHAT THAT BUYS ──────
#
# The scoring used to be inline, where the only way to exercise an arm was to run a
# break: a cargo run per arm, a clean worktree, and an edit to the tree. `outcome()`
# takes the harness's output text plus one flag and returns a verdict, so every arm
# is reachable from a string.
#
#     python3 .mise/tasks/replay-anchors --selftest   # edits nothing, runs no cargo
#
# Prefer that spelling over `mise run replay-anchors --selftest`: if a wrapper ever
# swallowed the flag, the task would fall through to a full replay, and a replay
# edits the tree. The dirty-tree refusal is the second guard against that, not the
# first.
#
# The synthetic transcripts are SHAPED on a real run, and the recorded sentence is
# spliced into them out of `REDNESS` rather than pasted. A pasted sentence would keep
# agreeing with itself after the registry moved, which is the rot this task hunts,
# reproduced inside its own self-test.
#
# **Watched, with the CONTROL first, because a red arm proves nothing until the green
# one is green.** Each line is one perturbation of a COPY of this file — never of the
# tree — and what that copy then printed:
#
#   * `outcome`'s `unaffected` arm with its `if not saw` test deleted, so a
#     declaration stops costing a watched line: `FAIL a declared row whose pass
#     sentence is absent fails` / `verdict unaffected: `unaffected` must still cost a
#     watched line`, exit 1.
#   * `outcome`'s `hang` arm with its `started(out)` test deleted, so a kill during a
#     build reads as a hang: `FAIL a kill before the runner started is NOT a hang` /
#     `verdict hang: a build that outran the timeout would read as evidence`, exit 1.
#   * `outcome`'s undeclared-green arm made to return `unaffected`: exit 1 on THREE
#     probes, not one - `FAIL an UNDECLARED green fails`, `FAIL a red at a DIFFERENT
#     sentence fails` and `FAIL an aborted anchor fails`, each `verdict unaffected`.
#     That is the arm's whole job in one line: a green break, a break red at another
#     assertion, and a break that never ran all arrive there, and the third was not
#     predicted when this record was written.
#
# The fifth element was also read out of a DRIFTED COPY of the registry through
# `REPLAY_REGISTRY`, and not only out of a synthetic tuple: a five-element `A5` was
# accepted and a six-element one printed the shape refusal. So the declaration is
# known to survive `ast.literal_eval` in a real row. `mise run anchors` reads
# `REDNESS` for its KEYS and its LENGTH and never unpacks a row, so the fifth element
# is invisible to that gate — checked line by line, because a row nobody can commit
# would make every arm above decoration.
#   * `run_watched`'s SIGTERM replaced by SIGKILL, so the restore trap is skipped:
#     `FAIL the killed shell ran its restore trap` / `the trap left '<no file>'`,
#     exit 1. The probe's own `sleep` ignores SIGTERM and still dies under the
#     group escalation, so this perturbation isolates the restore half.
#
# ── AND THE REPLAY IS IN NO GATE, A CORRECTNESS CLAIM AND NOT A SPEED ONE ──────
#
# The REPLAY edits the tree. `break-once.sh` restores a backup on EXIT, so a run
# racing an uncommitted edit restores OVER it — which is why this refuses a dirty
# worktree, and why it could not be a `pre-commit` citizen even if it were instant:
# that hook fires while a developer's working tree is live. It also runs one
# `cargo test` per row.
#
# So the honest place for it is by hand: at a merge gate, or by whoever rewrites a
# guard a record describes. Reviewer-run rather than author-run is the point — the
# author already ran it once, and that run is the thing being re-checked.
#
# `--selftest` is a different animal, and the heading above says REPLAY for that
# reason: it edits nothing, runs no cargo, and writes only under the system temp
# directory — a scratch directory and one output sink, and it removes both — so it is
# gate-safe on the same terms as `anchors-selftest`. **And it IS registered**, in
# `mise.toml`'s `gate` list, LAST among the steps there that need no compile and
# immediately above the first step that does. It sat higher, above `fmt-check`, until
# a reviewer timed the two: that list orders cheapest failures first, and this step
# cannot beat a step with no two-second floor. Deliberately NOT in `pre-commit`: that
# hook's filter tests for Rust and this file is not Rust, so there it would fire only
# by coincidence. The entry passes `args = ["--selftest"]` rather than a flag inside
# the task name, so nothing depends on how a wrapper parses a flag.
#
# What it checks is the CLASSIFIER and never a record. It cannot finish faster than
# the two seconds its kill probe waits, which is a floor read off the constant and
# not a timing measurement.
#
# The heading said "IT IS IN NO GATE" until one of its arms became a gate step. A
# heading that covers less than its words claim is the trap this file spends
# paragraphs on elsewhere, so the subject is now named in the heading itself.
#
#     mise run replay-anchors            # every row in REDNESS
#     mise run replay-anchors L5 A5      # named rows only
#
#     REPLAY_REGISTRY=/tmp/drifted mise run replay-anchors L5    # this task, tested
#     REPLAY_TIMEOUT=1200 mise run replay-anchors                # a slower machine
import ast
import os
import pathlib
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

ROOT = pathlib.Path(
    subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=True,
    ).stdout.strip()
)
# Overridable so THAT THIS TASK IS ITSELF TESTABLE: point it at a copy of the
# registry carrying a deliberately wrong sentence and the failure arm below must
# fire. Without the override the only way to reach that arm is to COMMIT a wrong
# value, and the tree must be clean before this will run at all — so the arm would
# stay unexercised, which for a checker is the same as absent.
TASK = pathlib.Path(os.environ.get("REPLAY_REGISTRY", ROOT / ".mise" / "tasks" / "anchors"))

try:
    SECONDS = int(os.environ.get("REPLAY_TIMEOUT", "600"))
except ValueError:
    sys.exit("REPLAY_TIMEOUT must be a whole number of seconds")
# Between the SIGTERM that lets `trap restore EXIT` run and the SIGKILL that does
# not. The measured trap took milliseconds; this is slack, and not a measurement.
GRACE = 10
# Read off the measured log, and conservative by direction — see the heading above.
STARTED = ("running ", "Finished ", "Starting ")


def registry(name: str):
    """One top-level list or dict from the anchors task, parsed and not executed."""
    for node in ast.parse(TASK.read_text()).body:
        if isinstance(node, ast.Assign) and getattr(node.targets[0], "id", "") == name:
            return ast.literal_eval(node.value)
    sys.exit(f"{TASK} has no top-level `{name}` to read")


ANCHORS = registry("ANCHORS")
REDNESS = registry("REDNESS")
# EXACTLY ONE row per id, and this used to be a dict comprehension - which keeps the
# LAST duplicate and drops the rest without a word. Measured on the registry at
# `d2f5e6e`: 113 rows, 111 ids, `L1` and `L2` twice each, and both resolving to a
# different FILE in a different crate than the row a reader finds first. This task
# would then cut a live literal out of the wrong crate and score whatever came back.
#
# `mise run anchors` refuses the same state, and it sits in BOTH the `pre-commit`
# and the `gate` list. This is not that check repeated for safety, for two separate
# reasons - one of them measured while writing this.
#
#   * `REPLAY_REGISTRY` points this task at an UNCOMMITTED file, which no hook has
#     seen. That is the path a drifted registry actually arrives by.
#   * **`pre-commit` does not fire on a commit that touches no Rust**, and a commit
#     that adds an `ANCHORS` row is exactly that commit. Read off `.githooks/
#     pre-commit`: it exits 0 before reaching `mise run pre-commit` unless a staged
#     path ends `.rs`, `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` or `rustfmt.toml`. So a duplicate
#     id can be COMMITTED ungated. `pre-push` runs `gate` with no such filter, so it
#     cannot be PUSHED - the window is between a commit and a push, and this task is
#     hand-run inside exactly that window.
rows: dict[str, tuple[str, str]] = {}
for record, _, target, literal in ANCHORS:
    if record in rows:
        sys.exit(f"{TASK} has more than one ANCHORS row with the id {record}. This "
                 "task resolves a REDNESS entry to a row by that id, so a repeated "
                 "one makes every row but the last unreachable - and the last may "
                 "name a different file. Give each row its own id.")
    rows[record] = (target, literal)

# A `REDNESS` id with no `ANCHORS` row, refused HERE and not as a bare `KeyError` at
# the lookup below. `mise run anchors` fails on the same state and names the same two
# fixes, and the wording follows it deliberately: this is the one task written for
# somebody who did not write the registry, so a traceback with no remedy is the worst
# place to spend their attention. A drifted `REPLAY_REGISTRY` reaches this too.
orphans = sorted(set(REDNESS) - set(rows))
if orphans:
    sys.exit(f"REDNESS entries with no ANCHORS row: {', '.join(orphans)}. This task "
             "reads the file and the search literal from ANCHORS, so those entries "
             "have nothing to cut. Either the row was dropped and the entry outlived "
             "it, or the id is misspelled.")


def exit_status(out: str) -> int | None:
    """What `break-once.sh` said the runner exited with, or None if it never got there.

    None is the aborted anchor and the killed run at once, and both belong in the
    failure arm for the same reason: no assertion was reached.
    """
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("── exit="):
            return int(line[len("── exit="):].split()[0])
    return None


def started(out: str) -> bool:
    """Did the runner get past its BUILD? The timeout heading says why that matters."""
    return any(line.strip().startswith(STARTED) for line in out.splitlines())


def selected(out: str) -> int | None:
    """How many tests the filter selected, as `break-once.sh` counted them.

    `None` is the harness's `?`, and it is NOT zero: no totals line was printed at
    all, which is an abort or a tree that did not compile. Zero is a filter that
    named nothing, and the arms below must be able to tell those two apart.

    The COUNT is not done here on purpose. `break-once.sh` does it once, for all
    three of its consumers, under either runner - see its own heading.
    """
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("── selected="):
            value = line[len("── selected="):].split()[0]
            return int(value) if value.isdigit() else None
    return None


def outcome(watched: str, unaffected: str | None, out: str,
            killed: bool) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Score one replayed break. Pure, so `--selftest` can reach every arm.

    Reads only `break-once.sh`'s output text and whether the wall clock killed it.
    Returns one of `red`, `hang`, `unaffected` or `fail`, and the reason to print.
    """
    saw = watched in out
    # ── EXACTLY ONE TEST, BEFORE ANY OTHER ARM ────────────────────────────────
    #
    # A `REDNESS` row names ONE guard, and `cargo test` resolves its filter as a
    # SUBSTRING - so a name that is a strict PREFIX of a sibling's selects both. A
    # run where only the SIBLING reddens then prints a panic, and every arm below
    # reads text: `red` would accept a sentence the named guard never printed.
    #
    # FIRST, and not folded into the arms, because it disqualifies the EVIDENCE
    # rather than scoring it. Which cell a row belongs in is unanswerable once the
    # output could have come from either of two tests, and that is as true of a
    # declared `unaffected` as of a `red`. The `unaffected` arm's `exit != 0` test
    # is a NARROWER version of this one that survives - it catches a two-test run
    # only when the sibling actually reddened, and only in that one cell.
    #
    # `None` falls through untouched. That is `── selected=?`, where the harness
    # found no totals line, and the arms below already report an abort and a
    # non-compiling tree correctly. Nine of the nine registered filters select one
    # today, measured, so this arm is PREVENTIVE and fires on nothing in this tree.
    picked = selected(out)
    if picked is not None and picked != 1:
        return "fail", (f"the filter selected {picked} tests, so no line in this run "
                        "can be attributed to the guard this row names")
    if unaffected:
        if killed:
            return "fail", ("this row DECLARES the guard unaffected, and the run had to "
                            "be killed — a break that hangs a guard is one it felt")
        if not saw:
            return "fail", ("the sentence this row records as its evidence that the "
                            "guard RAN is absent, so `unaffected` rests on nothing")
        status = exit_status(out)
        if status != 0:
            return "fail", (f"the row declares the guard unaffected and the run exited "
                            f"{status}: something under this filter caught the break")
        return "unaffected", unaffected
    if saw:
        return "red", watched
    if killed:
        if started(out):
            return "hang", "killed with the runner already past its build"
        return "fail", ("killed before the runner reached a test, so this is a BUILD "
                        "that outran REPLAY_TIMEOUT rather than a hang — or a runner "
                        "whose start line this task does not know")
    return "fail", "the recorded sentence is NOT in this run"


def declaration(record: str, row: tuple) -> str | None:
    """A row's optional fifth element: why this break cannot move the guard it names."""
    if len(row) == 4:
        return None
    if len(row) != 5 or not isinstance(row[4], str) or not row[4].strip():
        sys.exit(f"REDNESS row {record} has {len(row)} elements. A row is "
                 "(replacement, watched substring, test filter, extra runner args) and "
                 "may carry a FIFTH: a non-empty string saying why this break reaches "
                 "this guard and cannot move it. Nothing else is a row.")
    return row[4].strip()


def run_watched(argv: list[str], seconds: int) -> tuple[str, bool]:
    """Run argv under a wall-clock kill. Returns its merged output and whether it died.

    Output goes to a FILE rather than a pipe, because this function is the reader and
    the killer at once and a file needs no drain. The kill ORDER is measured — see the
    heading above; the short of it is that SIGKILL skips `break-once.sh`'s restore and
    a PID-only signal orphans the runner.
    """
    # `mkstemp` hands back an OPEN descriptor and a path, and this needs only the path:
    # the `with` below opens its own. Taking `[1]` and dropping the fd leaked one
    # descriptor per call, held until the process exited — one for a self-test and one
    # per row for a replay, so no run reached a limit and nothing would have reported
    # it either.
    fd, name = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="replay-anchors-")
    os.close(fd)
    sink = pathlib.Path(name)
    try:
        with sink.open("w") as handle:
            done = subprocess.Popen(argv, stdout=handle, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                                    cwd=ROOT, start_new_session=True)
        group = done.pid  # `start_new_session` makes the child its own group leader
        killed = False
        try:
            done.wait(timeout=seconds)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            killed = True
            try:
                os.killpg(group, signal.SIGTERM)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                pass
            try:
                done.wait(timeout=GRACE)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                pass
            # The group leader can exit after restoring the file while a child
            # ignores SIGTERM. Escalate the GROUP even when `done` is already
            # reaped; stopping at the leader is how an earlier run orphaned cargo.
            try:
                os.killpg(group, signal.SIGKILL)
            except ProcessLookupError:
                pass
            done.wait()
        return sink.read_text(errors="replace"), killed
    finally:
        sink.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def restored(record: str, killed: bool) -> None:
    """Refuse to run another break on a tree the last restore did not put back."""
    dirt = subprocess.run(["git", "status", "--porcelain"], capture_output=True,
                          text=True, check=True, cwd=ROOT).stdout
    if not dirt.strip():
        return
    print(f"\nSTOPPING after {record}: the tree was clean when this started, and it is "
          "not now.", file=sys.stderr)
    if killed:
        print("That row was killed on the wall clock, so the prime suspect is "
              "break-once.sh's `trap restore EXIT`, and the break may still be IN the "
              "file below. Restore it from git before running anything else.",
              file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        print("Either a restore failed, or something else wrote to this tree while the "
              "replay ran. Read the paths below before restoring anything: a checkout "
              "here would also discard whatever else is uncommitted.", file=sys.stderr)
    print(dirt, file=sys.stderr, end="")
    sys.exit(2)


def selftest() -> int:
    """Feed `outcome()` synthetic runs, and `run_watched()` a sleeping shell.

    Every case here is a guard, and the header above records what each one printed
    when it was watched failing. Nothing in this function reads or writes the
    repository — the kill probe works inside a scratch directory under the system
    temp, which is also why it is the one arm a dirty worktree does not block.
    """
    failures: list[str] = []

    def check(name: str, condition: bool, detail: str) -> None:
        if condition:
            print(f"ok: {name}")
        else:
            failures.append(name)
            print(f"FAIL {name}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"     {detail}", file=sys.stderr)

    # Shaped on the `break-once.sh` log measured 2026-08-08, with the sentence and the
    # filter spliced in from REDNESS so no fixture can agree with a registry that
    # moved. `build=False` is the run killed while cargo was still compiling.
    def transcript(body: str, status: int | None, build: bool = True,
                   picked: str = "1") -> str:
        head = "── broken: <target>\n── runner: cargo test\n"
        head += "   Compiling keylink v0.1.0 (<root>/keylink)\n"
        if build:
            head += ("    Finished `test` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] in 2.55s\n"
                     "     Running unittests src/lib.rs (target/debug/deps/keylink-<h>)\n\n")
        # `picked` is the harness's own `── selected=` line, and it is emitted only
        # beside an `── exit=` line because that is where the harness prints it: it
        # counts out of the log AFTER the run. `?` is the value for a run whose
        # totals line the harness could not find.
        tail = "" if status is None else (
            f"── exit={status} (non-zero means the test caught it)\n"
            f"── selected={picked}\n── log: <path>\n")
        return head + body + tail

    record = next(iter(REDNESS))
    sentence, test_filter = REDNESS[record][1], REDNESS[record][2]
    print(f"── the sentence and the filter come from REDNESS[{record!r}], read now")

    red = transcript(f"running 1 test\ntest {test_filter} ... FAILED\n\n"
                     f"thread '{test_filter}' panicked at <file>:<line>:\n{sentence}\n",
                     101)
    ok = transcript(f"running 1 test\ntest {test_filter} ... ok\n\n"
                    "test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 178 filtered out\n", 0)
    passed = f"test {test_filter} ... ok"

    # ── the control, and every probe under it is void until this one is green ─────
    verdict, why = outcome(sentence, None, red, killed=False)
    check("CONTROL a red at the recorded sentence scores red",
          (verdict, why) == ("red", sentence),
          f"verdict {verdict}, why: {why}. Every probe below is void until this "
          "passes: they share this fixture builder and this registry read.")

    # ── the distinction this task was written for: red, but somewhere else ────────
    elsewhere = transcript(f"running 1 test\ntest {test_filter} ... FAILED\n\n"
                           "assertion `left == right` failed: something else entirely\n",
                           101)
    check("the wrong-sentence fixture does not contain the recorded sentence",
          sentence not in elsewhere,
          "the fixture carries the sentence it exists to lack, so the probe under it "
          "would pass for the wrong reason")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, elsewhere, killed=False)
    check("a red at a DIFFERENT sentence fails", verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}")

    # ── an aborted anchor: no `exit=` line anywhere in the run ────────────────────
    aborted = ("BREAK HARNESS ABORT: expected exactly one occurrence, found 0 in "
               "<target>: '<literal>'\n")
    check("an aborted anchor has no exit status to read", exit_status(aborted) is None,
          f"exit_status read {exit_status(aborted)} out of a run that never printed one")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, aborted, killed=False)
    check("an aborted anchor fails", verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}")

    # ── green and undeclared: `unaffected` must not be reachable by default ───────
    check("the green fixture reports exit=0", exit_status(ok) == 0,
          f"exit_status read {exit_status(ok)}, so the probes under it are void")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, ok, killed=False)
    check("an UNDECLARED green fails", verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}")

    # ── green and declared: the third outcome, and what it still costs ────────────
    reason = "the depths are compared against each other"
    verdict, why = outcome(passed, reason, ok, killed=False)
    check("a green DECLARED unaffected passes, carrying its reason",
          (verdict, why) == ("unaffected", reason), f"verdict {verdict}, why: {why}")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, reason, ok, killed=False)
    check("a declared row whose pass sentence is absent fails", verdict == "fail",
          f"verdict {verdict}: `unaffected` must still cost a watched line")
    # `picked="?"` on purpose, and it is what keeps this probe measuring the arm it
    # was written for. This fixture holds TWO tests, so a `── selected=2` would be
    # caught one arm earlier and the `exit != 0` test below it would stop being
    # exercised at all - an assertion passing for a reason it does not name. `?` is
    # the harness's own value for a run it could not count, which is the state this
    # arm has to survive: the sibling arm is the NARROW guard that still works when
    # the count is unavailable.
    sibling = transcript(f"running 2 tests\ntest {test_filter} ... ok\n"
                         "test a_sibling_under_the_same_filter ... FAILED\n", 101,
                         picked="?")
    check("the sibling fixture carries no usable count", selected(sibling) is None,
          f"selected read {selected(sibling)}, so the exactly-one arm would score "
          "this fixture and the exit-status arm under it would go unexercised")
    verdict, _ = outcome(passed, reason, sibling, killed=False)
    check("a declared row is refused when a sibling reddened", verdict == "fail",
          f"verdict {verdict}: exit={exit_status(sibling)} says the break was observed")

    # ── the count itself: a filter that named two guards disqualifies the run ─────
    #
    # The registered filters all select one today, so nothing in the tree reaches
    # this. It is the arm that makes a record naming a PREFIX of a sibling's name
    # fail instead of reading as evidence.
    two = transcript(f"running 2 tests\ntest {test_filter} ... ok\n"
                     "test a_sibling_under_the_same_filter ... FAILED\n"
                     f"thread 'a_sibling_under_the_same_filter' panicked:\n{sentence}\n",
                     101, picked="2")
    check("the two-test fixture reports a count of 2", selected(two) == 2,
          f"selected read {selected(two)}, so the probes under it are void")
    check("the recorded sentence IS in the two-test fixture", sentence in two,
          "the fixture lacks the sentence, so the probe below would pass without "
          "the count doing any work")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, two, killed=False)
    check("a run whose filter selected TWO fails, sentence or no sentence",
          verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}: the sentence came from a sibling")
    # GREEN and two, so the count is the ONLY thing that can refuse it. The fixture
    # above exits 101, which the declared arm's own `exit != 0` test already
    # catches - a probe run against it would pass with this arm deleted, which is a
    # probe measuring the wrong guard. Watched: with the arm cut, this one fails and
    # that one does not.
    two_green = transcript(f"running 2 tests\ntest {test_filter} ... ok\n"
                           "test a_sibling_under_the_same_filter ... ok\n", 0,
                           picked="2")
    verdict, _ = outcome(passed, reason, two_green, killed=False)
    check("a DECLARED row whose filter selected two fails, even all-green",
          verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}: `unaffected` needs one guard too")
    check("the control fixture reports a count of 1", selected(red) == 1,
          f"selected read {selected(red)}: the control passes only because the count "
          "is right, not because it is missing")
    verdict, _ = outcome(passed, reason, transcript("running 1 test\n", None), killed=True)
    check("a declared row that HANGS fails", verdict == "fail", f"verdict {verdict}")

    # ── the hang, and the build that must never be mistaken for one ───────────────
    hung = transcript("running 1 test\n", None)
    check("the hang fixture has no exit status", exit_status(hung) is None,
          "a killed run cannot have printed one, so this fixture is wrong")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, hung, killed=True)
    check("a timeout with the runner past its build is scored as break OBSERVED",
          verdict == "hang", f"verdict {verdict}")
    building = transcript("", None, build=False)
    check("the building fixture shows no start line", not started(building),
          "the fixture already looks like a started run, so the probe under it is void")
    verdict, _ = outcome(sentence, None, building, killed=True)
    check("a kill before the runner started is NOT a hang", verdict == "fail",
          f"verdict {verdict}: a build that outran the timeout would read as evidence")

    # ── the row shape, refused where no cargo run has been spent yet ──────────────
    check("a four-element row declares nothing",
          declaration("X", ("a", "b", "c", [])) is None,
          "an ordinary row grew a declaration out of nowhere")
    for bad in (("a", "b", "c", [], "why", "extra"), ("a", "b", "c", [], ""),
                ("a", "b", "c", [], True)):
        try:
            declaration("X", bad)
            check(f"a malformed row is refused: {bad!r}", False, "it was accepted")
        except SystemExit as refusal:
            check(f"a malformed row is refused: {bad!r}",
                  "Nothing else is a row" in str(refusal),
                  f"refused with the wrong message: {refusal}")

    # ── the kill itself, against a sleeping shell shaped like break-once.sh ───────
    #
    # The arms above are strings, and a string cannot show that the signal reached the
    # runner or that the restore ran. This one drives `run_watched` for real. The pid
    # file is what makes the GROUP half checkable: the shell's child has to be gone
    # too, not just the shell. The child deliberately ignores SIGTERM, proving the
    # SIGKILL escalation still reaches the group after its leader restored and exited.
    scratch = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="replay-anchors-selftest-"))
    marker, pidfile, ready = (scratch / "trap-ran", scratch / "child-pid",
                              scratch / "ready")
    probe = scratch / "sleeping-break.sh"
    try:
        probe.write_text("#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n"
                         f'restore() {{ echo RESTORED >> "{marker}"; }}\n'
                         "trap restore EXIT\n"
                         'echo "── broken: <nothing at all>"\n'
                         f"bash -c 'trap \"\" TERM; echo READY > \"{ready}\"; "
                         "exec sleep 120' &\n"
                         f'echo "$!" > "{pidfile}"\n'
                         "wait\n")
        probe.chmod(0o755)
        out, killed = run_watched([str(probe)], 2)
        check("the wall-clock kill fires", killed,
              f"run_watched returned killed={killed} for a shell that sleeps 120 s")
        check("the killed run's partial output is kept", "── broken:" in out,
              f"the sink held {out!r}, so a hang would be scored with nothing to read")
        armed = ready.read_text().strip() if ready.exists() else "<no file>"
        check("the child was armed to ignore the graceful signal", armed == "READY",
              f"the readiness marker held {armed!r}, so the escalation was not tested")
        trap = marker.read_text().strip() if marker.exists() else "<no file>"
        check("the killed shell ran its restore trap", trap == "RESTORED",
              f"the trap left {trap!r}: a break killed this way would stay in the file")
        child = int(pidfile.read_text().strip()) if pidfile.exists() else 0
        # POLLED, not sampled once. `SIGKILL` to a group is delivered
        # synchronously and reaped ASYNCHRONOUSLY, so for a short window after
        # the kill the pid still answers `kill(pid, 0)` — the process is gone
        # and its slot is not. Sampling once turned that window into a gate
        # failure: this guard failed inside a full `pre-push` run and passed on
        # a re-run seconds later, which is the shape that trains people to
        # re-run a gate instead of reading it.
        #
        # Two seconds is far longer than a reap and far shorter than the 120 s
        # the child would sleep for if the kill had genuinely missed it, so the
        # guard still fails loudly for the fault it is about.
        alive = True
        for _ in range(200):
            try:
                os.kill(child, 0)
            except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
                alive = False
                break
            time.sleep(0.01)
        check("the signal reached the shell's child, not only the shell", not alive,
              f"pid {child} is still running, so a killed row would leave the runner "
              "behind")
    finally:
        # Removed, and NOT kept the way `break-once.sh` keeps its log. That log is the
        # evidence a reader's conclusion rests on; this directory holds a sleeping
        # shell and a pid, and every failure detail above already prints what it read
        # out of them, so removing it destroys no evidence. Twenty-seven of these had
        # accumulated under one TMPDIR before this line existed, one per run — and
        # since `--selftest` became a gate step that is one per push, per worktree.
        shutil.rmtree(scratch, ignore_errors=True)

    print()
    if failures:
        print(f"{len(failures)} of the classifier's guards failed: "
              f"{', '.join(failures)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print("ok: every arm scored as recorded — red, a red at the wrong sentence, an "
          "aborted anchor, an undeclared green, a declared one, a filter that "
          "selected two, both sides of the timeout, the row shape, and the kill "
          "itself")
    return 0


argv = sys.argv[1:]
if any(arg.lstrip("-") == "selftest" for arg in argv):
    if len(argv) != 1:
        sys.exit("--selftest takes no row names: it replays nothing and reads no rows")
    sys.exit(selftest())

wanted = argv
unknown = sorted(set(wanted) - set(REDNESS))
if unknown:
    sys.exit(f"no REDNESS entry for: {', '.join(unknown)}")
plan = [record for record in REDNESS if not wanted or record in wanted]
# Every row's shape, checked before the first cargo run rather than after the eighth.
declared = {record: declaration(record, REDNESS[record]) for record in plan}

# Refused rather than worked around. `break-once.sh`'s `trap restore EXIT` copies a
# backup back over the file it edited, so anything uncommitted in that file is lost —
# and it is lost silently, since the restore is the harness working as designed.
dirty = subprocess.run(
    ["git", "status", "--porcelain"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True, cwd=ROOT
).stdout
if dirty.strip():
    print("refusing to run against a dirty tree: break-once.sh restores a backup on",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("EXIT, which would overwrite whatever is uncommitted here.", file=sys.stderr)
    print(dirty, file=sys.stderr, end="")
    sys.exit(2)

failed: list[str] = []
scored: dict[str, list[str]] = {"red": [], "hang": [], "unaffected": []}
for record in plan:
    replacement, watched, test_filter, extra = REDNESS[record][:4]
    target, literal = rows[record]
    print(f"════ {record}  {test_filter}")
    out, killed = run_watched(
        [str(ROOT / "scripts" / "break-once.sh"), target, literal, replacement,
         test_filter, *extra],
        SECONDS,
    )
    # EVERY line the harness prints for itself, and none of the runner's. It tags its
    # own with `── `, so that prefix is the whole rule - and it replaces a list of
    # four startswith tests that had to grow by hand whenever the harness grew a
    # line. The `── selected=` count and the remedy beside it were invisible here on
    # their first draft for exactly that reason. `ABORT` and `expected exactly one`
    # stay listed because the abort goes to stderr WITHOUT the prefix.
    #
    # Never the whole test log: the harness keeps that in the file its `── log:` line
    # names, and the failure arm below sends the reader there.
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if ("ABORT" in line or "expected exactly one" in line
                or line.startswith("── ")):
            print(f"     {line}")
    if killed:
        # What was SENT, not what ended it: SIGTERM goes first so the restore runs,
        # and a row that outlived the grace was ended by SIGKILL with no restore. The
        # check below is what tells those two apart, so this line does not guess.
        print(f"     ── killed after {SECONDS}s (REPLAY_TIMEOUT): SIGTERM to the "
              "process group, then SIGKILL if it outlived the grace")
    # Before the verdict, because a verdict on a tree the harness left broken is not
    # worth reading, and the next row would cut into that broken file.
    restored(record, killed)

    verdict, why = outcome(watched, declared[record], out, killed)
    if verdict == "red":
        print(f"     ✓ still red at: {why}")
        if killed:
            print("       AND it had to be killed, so something after that sentence "
                  "does not finish under this break.")
        scored["red"].append(record)
        continue
    if verdict == "hang":
        print(f"     ✓ the break was OBSERVED: {why}")
        print("       No assertion reported, so the recorded sentence is UNCHECKED for")
        print("       this row - a hang says the guard felt the break, and not where it")
        print("       would have failed. break-once.sh names its log after the test")
        print("       filter under $TMPDIR (its `log=$(mktemp -t ...)` line); the kill")
        print("       landed before it could echo that path.")
        scored["hang"].append(record)
        continue
    if verdict == "unaffected":
        print(f"     ✓ reached and unaffected, as this row declares: {why}")
        print("       The recorded sentence proves the guard RAN. Nothing here says a")
        print("       break can redden it, and nothing in this task ever will.")
        scored["unaffected"].append(record)
        continue

    failed.append(record)
    print(f"     ✗ {why}")
    if declared[record]:
        print("       This row DECLARES the guard unaffected, for this reason:")
        print(f"         {declared[record]}")
        print("       Either that reason stopped being true, or the line it records as")
        print("       evidence that the guard runs has moved. Do not delete the")
        print("       declaration to make this green.")
    else:
        print("       Expected to find:")
        print(f"         {watched}")
        print("       A break that went green, one whose anchor aborted, one that no")
        print("       longer compiles, and one red at a DIFFERENT assertion all land")
        print("       here. Read the log at the `── log:` path above - an ABORT line")
        print("       and no log path means the harness refused before it ran anything,")
        print("       and the ABORT says why. Then fix whichever of the two is wrong -")
        print("       the record or the guard.")
        print("       Do NOT relax the expected sentence to match the run without")
        print("       reading why it changed: a break red at another assertion is a")
        print("       record describing coverage it no longer has.")
        if exit_status(out) == 0:
            print("       This run went GREEN. If the break genuinely cannot move this")
            print("       guard, that is the third outcome and it is DECLARED: add a")
            print("       fifth element to the row saying why, and record a line from")
            print("       the green run as the `watched` sentence so the row still")
            print("       proves the guard RAN. An undeclared green stays a failure.")

print()
if failed:
    print(f"{len(failed)} of {len(plan)} replayed breaks were not the outcome their row "
          f"records: {', '.join(failed)}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)
print(f"ok: {len(plan)} of the {len(REDNESS)} REDNESS rows replayed - "
      f"{len(scored['red'])} printed the recorded sentence, "
      f"{len(scored['hang'])} were scored on a wall-clock kill, "
      f"{len(scored['unaffected'])} are declared unaffected and passed")
if scored["hang"]:
    print(f"    the recorded sentence is UNCHECKED for {', '.join(scored['hang'])}: a "
          "kill says the break was observed, and not where the guard fails")
if scored["unaffected"]:
    print(f"    {', '.join(scored['unaffected'])} assert that the guard RAN and assert "
          "nothing about redness - a declared reason is all that holds them")
print(f"    REDNESS covers {len(REDNESS)} of the {len(ANCHORS)} registered rows - "
      "`mise run anchors` prints that ratio, and the rest are unreplayed")
